( net-echo.tal — the step-1 gate ROM for the CONNECT-only network device (0xd0). )
(                                                                                        )
( It is the SMALLEST program that can prove the device does what it claims: it dials an )
( address it was handed, sends bytes, and reads back what the FAR NODE made of them. )
( Anything less — a connect that is never written to, a read that is never compared — )
( proves reachability, not a round trip. )
(                                                                                        )
( input  : two lines on stdin. line 1 = the URI, colon-separated 'tcp:host:port'. )
(          line 2 = the payload; a newline is appended so a line-oriented peer flushes. )
( output : the reply bytes verbatim, halt rc 0. )
(          "NA" + newline, halt rc 2 — on a refused/failed connect, or on an empty read. )
(                                                                                        )
( NA IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE LINK, never a substitute for one. The device leaves a failed )
( connect readable as Disconnected [it never lets a failure read as Connected], so this )
( ROM can distinguish "no link" from "link, no data" and refuses in both rather than )
( printing something that looks like a reply. )
(                                                                                        )
( The read is BLOCKING with the device's own deadline behind it, so there is no state )
( spin here: a peer that says nothing costs one timeout and comes back with length 0, )
( which is a refusal, not a hang. )
(                                                                                        )
( zero-page: state=00 ptr=02 len=04 i=06 got=08 )

|0100
    ;on-console #10 DEO2
    ;uri-buf #02 STZ2        ( the first line accumulates into the URI buffer )
    BRK

@on-console
    #12 DEI
    DUP #0a EQU ,&nl JCN
    #02 LDZ2 STA             ( byte -> ram[ptr] )
    #02 LDZ2 INC2 #02 STZ2
    BRK
    &nl
    POP
    #00 LDZ #00 EQU ,&first JCN
    ;send JMP2
    &first
    ;dial JMP2

@dial
    #00 #02 LDZ2 STA         ( NUL-terminate the URI in place )
    ;uri-buf #d2 DEO2        ( the low byte of the addr port IS the connect trigger )
    #da DEI #02 NEQ ,&bad JCN
    ;pay-buf #02 STZ2        ( the second line accumulates into the payload buffer )
    #01 #00 STZ
    BRK
    &bad ;na JMP2            ( Disconnected: the device refused, and said why on stderr )

@send
    #0a #02 LDZ2 STA         ( append the newline the reader stripped )
    #02 LDZ2 INC2 #02 STZ2
    #02 LDZ2 ;pay-buf SUB2 #04 STZ2        ( len = ptr - pay-buf )
    #04 LDZ2 #d4 DEO2                      ( set the transfer length )
    ;pay-buf #d8 DEO2                      ( the low byte of the write port sends it )
    #d4 DEI2 #04 LDZ2 NEQ2 ,&short JCN     ( a SHORT WRITE is not a success )
    #0100 #d4 DEO2                         ( read up to 256 bytes )
    ;in-buf #d6 DEO2                       ( the low byte of the read port receives )
    #d4 DEI2 #08 STZ2                      ( got = the length the device actually moved )
    #08 LDZ2 #0000 EQU2 ,&empty JCN
    #0000 #06 STZ2
    &loop
    ;in-buf #06 LDZ2 ADD2 LDA #18 DEO
    #06 LDZ2 INC2 #06 STZ2
    #06 LDZ2 #08 LDZ2 NEQ2 ,&loop JCN
    #80 #0f DEO              ( halt, exit 0 )
    BRK
    &short ;na JMP2
    &empty ;na JMP2

@na
    ;na-str
    &l LDAk #00 EQU ,&d JCN
    LDAk #18 DEO INC2 ,&l JMP
    &d POP2
    #82 #0f DEO              ( halt, exit 2 — honest n/a, never a fabricated reply )
    BRK

@na-str "NA 0a 00

@uri-buf $200
@pay-buf $200
@in-buf  $200
